# vendor/ocr-models/en_dict.txt's bytes are SHA256-pinned in models.ts and re-verified at
# process start (MUST-3.11, MUST-3.12). core.autocrlf=true with no override here would
# rewrite it to CRLF on a Windows checkout, changing its hash and failing verification.
# NOTICE carries the same risk in principle, pinned here too for consistency. The three
# .onnx files are already binary-detected by git; declared explicitly so that never changes.
vendor/ocr-models/en_dict.txt -text
vendor/ocr-models/NOTICE -text
vendor/ocr-models/*.onnx -text

# A fresh Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true otherwise checks these out with CRLF line
# endings, which breaks their #!/bin/bash shebang the moment they run under bash and fails the
# three shebang assertions in tests/ops/install.test.ts. Forcing LF here is what every other
# platform already gets by default.
*.sh text eol=lf
